 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to electronic\npayment of fees of brand label registration and to change the time required\nfor an application to be deemed approved",
    "basePrintNo": "S7821",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7821-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §107-a, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7821\n\nSPONSOR: MURPHY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in\nrelation to electronic payment of fees of brand label registration and\nto change the time required for an application to be deemed approved\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would amend the Alcoholic Beverage Control Law (\"ABCL\") to\npermit brand label registration applications to submit their processing\nfee charged by the State Liquor Authority (SLA) electronically. In addi-\ntion, this bill would eliminate the need for a wait period for a brand\nlabel to be deemed approved for brand name labels that have already been\napproved by the Alcohol and Tobacco Tax and Trade Bureau.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis section of the law was amended in 2017 to allow for the electronic\nfiling of brand label registration. However, applicants are still\nrequired to submit the application fee by check or draft. This bill\nwould allow applicants to submit their fees to the State Liquor Authori-\nty via electronically.\n\nIn addition, currently brand label applicants who have already had their\nbrand or trade name label approved by the Alcohol and Tobacco Tax and\nTrade Bureau of the United States Department of Treasury are deemed\nregistered and approved by the authority so long as the application is\nin the proper format and the SLA does not deny such application within\nthirty days. This timeframe has proven to be a burden for applicants\nwith numerous brand labels who have already been approved at the federal\nlevel. In addition, the SLA rarely denies such applicants because of\ntheir prior approval.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAll brand labels have to be registered and approved along with a fee by\nthe State Liquor Authority. Larger breweries do so on a national level.\nSince new brand labels have already been federal approved by the Alcohol\nTax, Tobacco and Trade Bureau, it makes little sense to have a thirty-\nday waiting period when such federal approvals are auto-approved at the\nstate level.\n\nAdditionally, such filings are accompanied by payment of a small fee.\nExisting law only allows for payment by check. Authorizing electronic\npayment will further expedite the process and spur economic development\nand distribution in New York State.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
